course: world
lesson: 39
title: First aid and safety
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: First aid is the help you give someone straight away, before a doctor comes. The first rule is to keep yourself safe, and the second is to get help from an adult or call the emergency number: 911 in the USA and Canada, 999 in the UK and Ireland (where 112 also works), 000 in Australia and 111 in New Zealand.
explain: A burn should be cooled under cool running water for about 20 minutes. Never put ice, butter or cream on it: ice damages the skin further and butter keeps the heat in.
explain: For a cut that bleeds, press a clean cloth firmly on it to stop the bleeding. For a nosebleed, lean forward, not back, and pinch the soft part of the nose.
explain: On the road, cross at a crossing, stop, look and listen both ways, and wear bright or reflective clothes in the dark so drivers can see you.
explain: In a fire, get out fast and stay low, because smoke rises and the cleaner air is near the floor. Never go back inside. If your clothes catch fire: stop, drop and roll.
fact: When giving first aid, first make sure you are safe yourself.
fact: The emergency number is 911 in the USA and Canada and 999 in the UK and Ireland, where 112 also works.
fact: The emergency number is 000 in Australia and 111 in New Zealand.
fact: Australians often call their country Oz for short.
fact: When calling for help, say what happened and where you are, and do not hang up until told to.
fact: A burn should be cooled under cool running water for about 20 minutes, never with ice, butter or cream.
fact: Pressing a clean cloth firmly on a cut helps stop the bleeding.
fact: For a nosebleed, lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose.
fact: Smoke rises, so the cleanest air in a fire is near the floor.
fact: If your clothes catch fire, stop, drop and roll.
fact: Never go back into a burning building.
fact: Cross the road at a crossing and look and listen both ways.
fact: Bright or reflective clothes help drivers see you in the dark.
exercise: What is the first rule of first aid? | Make sure you are safe yourself | Run away and tell no one what you saw | Give the hurt person food and a drink | Move the hurt person somewhere else at once
exercise: A boy in Ohio, in the USA, sees a bad car crash. Which number should he call? | 911 | 999 | 000 | 111
exercise: What should you say when you call for help? | What happened and where you are | Only your own name and how old you are | Only what the weather is like outside | Nothing at all, just breathe and wait silently
exercise: How should a burn be treated? | Cool it under cool running water for about 20 minutes | Rub butter on it | Put ice straight on it | Cover it with cream at once
exercise: Why should you never put butter on a burn? | It keeps the heat in the skin | It makes the burn far too cold | It washes the burn too much | It is too expensive
exercise: What helps stop the bleeding from a cut? | Pressing a clean cloth firmly on it | Blowing on it hard until it dries up | Putting it under very hot running water | Shaking the hand fast to throw the blood off
exercise: What should you do for a nosebleed? | Lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose | Lean back and look at the ceiling | Lie flat and blow the nose hard | Jump up and down
exercise: Why should you stay low when escaping a fire? | Smoke rises, so cleaner air is near the floor | Fire cannot burn near the floor | It is faster to crawl than to walk, even for grown-ups | So the fire cannot see you
exercise: What should you do if your clothes catch fire? | Stop, drop and roll | Run as fast as you can | Fan the flames with your hands | Jump up and down
exercise: What should you do after you escape from a burning house? | Stay outside and never go back in | Go back to fetch your toys | Go back in quickly to close all the windows | Hide in the garden shed
exercise: Where is the safest place to cross the road? | At a crossing | Between two parked cars | On a bend in the road | Anywhere you can run fast
exercise: What should you do before crossing a road? | Stop, look and listen both ways | Look only to the left, then step out | Close your eyes and run across fast | Put your music on loud and keep walking
exercise: Why should you wear bright or reflective clothes at night? | So drivers can see you | So you stay warmer | So the road is lit for others | So cars drive faster
exercise: Why should you not use ice on a burn? | Ice can damage the skin even more | Ice makes the burn heal much too fast | Ice is too soft to help | Ice turns the burn into a cut
test: Your little brother touches a hot iron and burns his finger. What do you do first? | Hold the finger under cool running water and tell an adult | Rub butter on the finger | Press a block of ice on the finger | Wrap it tight and say nothing
test: You see a man lying still on a busy road. What is the right first thing? | Check the traffic is safe, then call for help | Run straight into the traffic to him | Walk away because it is not your job | Try to carry him on your own across the busy road
test: A friend falls and cuts her knee and it bleeds. What helps most? | Press a clean cloth firmly on the cut | Shake the leg hard to dry the blood off quickly | Wash the cut in a dirty puddle | Make her run to stop the blood
test: The fire alarm goes off and the corridor is full of smoke. How do you move? | Low, close to the floor | Standing tall on tiptoe | Slowly, carrying your bag | Back to your bed to hide
test: A girl in London calls 999 after an accident. Why should she not hang up first? | The helper may need more information from you | It costs less money to stay on the line than to call again | The ambulance follows the phone line | Hanging up sends the call to the police
test: Your cousin's nose bleeds at dinner. What should he do? | Lean forward and pinch the soft part of his nose | Tip his head far back | Lie down on the floor | Blow his nose hard again and again
test: Walking home in winter darkness, what keeps you safest from cars? | Wearing something bright or reflective | Wearing a dark coat so drivers are not dazzled | Walking right down the middle of the road | Walking with your eyes on your phone screen
test: Why is a crossing safer than running between parked cars? | Drivers expect people there and can see them | Cars are not allowed on any road with a crossing | Parked cars always move away | Running is safer than walking
test: A girl in Sydney, Australia, finds her grandad has fallen and cannot get up. Which number should she call? | 000 | 911 | 999 | 111
test: Which is true? | Smoke from a fire is dangerous to breathe | You should go back for pets in a burning house | You should hide under the bed in a fire | Fire never spreads to a second room
test: burnt my hand on the oven ugh what do i do | Cool it under cool running water for about 20 minutes | Put ice straight on it | Rub butter on it | Wrap it in a warm towel
test: in oz, someone's hurt bad, what number | 000 | 911 | 999 | 111
test: my cousin says tip your head back for a nosebleed. true? | No, lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose | Yes, tip back so the blood stays in | Yes, and lie down flat too | No, blow your nose hard
test: Tom reckons if there's smoke you should stand up tall and run. right? | No, smoke rises, so stay low where the air is cleaner | Yes, standing tall keeps your head above all of the smoke | Yes, the clean air is near the ceiling | No, stay still and hold your breath
exam: Identify the first consideration when administering first aid. | The assurance of one's own safety | The removal of the casualty to hospital | The administration of medicine | The washing of one's hands
exam: Identify the correct treatment of a burn. | Cooling under cool running water for about twenty minutes | Application of ice directly to the skin | Application of butter to the skin | Covering immediately with cream
exam: Identify the principal reason for which one should keep low to the floor in a fire. | Smoke rises, and the cleanest air lies near the floor | The floor is cooler than the flames | Fire does not burn near the floor, only higher up the walls | It is quicker to crawl than to walk
exam: Identify the correct treatment of a nosebleed. | Leaning forward and pinching the soft part of the nose | Leaning back and raising the head | Lying flat upon the back | Blowing the nose forcefully
